  }
  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Concealing Default Views**\n\nI\'m working on hiding the \"Project,\" \"Team,\" and \"Credit\" sections by default. I plan to reveal these only when the user clicks their corresponding links in the navigation. The intention is to streamline the initial view and make it clearer.\n\n\n**Refining Section Transitions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on transitions between sections. \"Start the Journey\" will bypass Project, Team, and Credit, jumping straight to the map. The little boat will move into the map upon the journey\'s start. Initially, the map will be grayscale, with the sea a consistent blue. Countries NL, DE, ES, PT, FR, and IT will color one-by-one with dark amaranth. The boat\'s movement will trigger a progress bar.\n\n\n**Developing Visual Effects**\n\nMy current focus is on the map\'s visual effects. I will begin with an uncolored map, then highlight countries individually with a dark amaranth. The boat\'s descent into the map is next, and its movement will activate a progress bar to the left. Finally, I will create an \"Analysis Section\" after the map with a blue background, placeholder text, and a chart. I\'ve decided to make the \"About\" section an overlay.\n\n\n**Considering About\'s Position**\n\nI\'m revisiting the placement of the \"About\" section. Clicking \"Start the Journey\" should scroll to the map, but the \"About\" section is currently below the map. I\'m considering making \"About\" an absolutely positioned overlay within `App` or `Hero`, perhaps as a modal with a close button to appear on click. I\'ll modify the `Navigation` component to accommodate an `onSelect` prop or leverage context/state to trigger the modal. Ultimately, I\'ll rewrite `App.tsx` to manage this state.\n\n\n**Implementing About Modal**\n\nI\'ve integrated an `AboutModal` component, modeled after the `About` section, using a state variable `activeAboutTab` in `App.tsx` to handle its visibility. The `Hero` component now receives an `onOpenAbout` function, which is passed down to `Navigation`. Clicking \"Project\" in navigation sets `activeAboutTab` and triggers the modal. I\'m building out the new component now, and will soon include the other links.\n\n\n**Expanding Navigation Logic**\n\nI\'ve integrated the `Team` and `Credit` buttons into the navigation. Clicking now triggers the `AboutModal` with corresponding content. I\'m focusing on ensuring these links function smoothly alongside the existing \"Project\" link. I\'ll continue refining the `Navigation` component and its integration with the modal.\n\n\n**Refining Sea and Land Colors**\n\nI\'ve clarified the color scheme for the map. I\'ll use `#1A3A5F` for the sea, matching the editorial design\'s country text color, and `#FBF9F5` or `#EAE5DF` for unhighlighted land. The highlighted countries will be colored with dark amaranth, `#6D213C`.\n\nI\'m now determining how to handle the boat\'s transformation into a progress bar during scrolling. I will integrate this into the scroll sequence.\n\n\n**Defining Scroll Interactions**\n\nI\'m now determining the interplay between scroll and the map\'s visual elements. My plan involves a sticky map section that triggers an animation sequence, or perhaps simply a state that advances when in view. The boat\'s role as a progress bar is key. After the map animation, the user will scroll to an \"AnalysisSection\". Alternatively, I could make the boat fixed on the left as a visual progress bar.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 11
      nanos: 588382558
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
