  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Visualizing Map Journey**\n\nI\'m now diving into `MapJourney.tsx`. My focus is on animating the boat\'s entry and the subsequent country highlighting. Currently, I\'m conceptualizing the initial grey/white map with a blue sea. My goal is a smooth transition where countries sequentially illuminate in `#6D213C` as the journey progresses.\n\n\n**Implementing Navigation Progress**\n\nI\'m now integrating a navigation progress bar, visualized by the boat\'s movement to the left. Using `framer-motion`\'s `useScroll` in `MapJourney.tsx` is being explored for driving the animation, though a state-based `useEffect` triggered by `whileInView` and `onViewportEnter` seems more promising. I\'m modifying `MapJourney.tsx` to include `HIGHLIGHTED_COUNTRIES` and manage a `coloredCount` state to control country highlighting during the scroll.\n\n\n**Refining Navigation Strategy**\n\nI\'m reconsidering the boat\'s positioning and navigation bar. It\'s becoming apparent that managing the boat\'s fixed position and scroll-driven animation within `App.tsx` or a wrapper is more straightforward. This approach facilitates a unified control over the entire sequence. Specifically, a navigation progress bar with a fixed boat icon on the left is ideal during the \"AnalysisSection\" segment. The map sequence is broken down into four key steps for development.\n\n\n**Revising Animation Trigger**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a scroll-triggered animation within `MapJourney.tsx`. I\'m using `framer-motion`\'s `useScroll` to drive the animation. The idea is to have a tall container where scrolling progresses the animation - the boat drops in, countries color sequentially, the boat moves left, then the map fades out. This approach aligns with creating a navigation progress bar using scroll position as the driver. I\'m exploring setting `min-height: 200vh` on the container and using a `useRef` to target the animation.\n\n\n**Simplifying Animation Logic**\n\nI\'m now shifting gears to simplify the animation triggers. Considering that scroll-tied map animations could feel clunky if users scroll too fast, I\'m now aiming for time-based animation for the map. Once the map animation is complete, I\'ll set a flag, `isAnimationComplete`, and then the boat will transition to the left. I\'m thinking of placing a fixed boat in `App.tsx` and showing it either after the map animation ends or after scrolling past the map section.\n\n\n**Sequencing Animations Precisely**\n\nI\'m now implementing the detailed animation sequence within `MapJourney.tsx`, managing `coloredCount` and `boatPhase` states. I\'ve initiated `setTimeout` calls for sequenced animations, starting with the boat falling, then country highlighting triggered by `setTimeout`, and transitioning the boat onto the map. I\'m focusing on perfectly aligning the animation durations.\n\n\n**Synchronizing Animation Timings**\n\nI\'m now refining the animation logic, managing state variables like `isInView` to control animation triggers. I\'m leveraging `setTimeout` calls within `useEffect` to sequence the boat\'s entry, country highlighting, and boat movement across the map. My goal is precise timing for each stage, and this includes cleanup to handle component unmounting. The `useMemo` hook is being used for the projection.\n\n\n**Adjusting Highlight Logic**\n\nI\'m now focusing on optimizing the country highlighting within `MapJourney.tsx`. I\'m fine-tuning the `isHighlighted` condition, ensuring accurate color transitions and the right stroke properties. My goal is pixel-perfect visual synchronization with the boat\'s movement and position.\n\n\n**Refining Timeline Control**\n\nI\'m now streamlining the timeline progression in `MapJourney.tsx`, managing animation state more directly. I\'ve removed `setTimeout` and am directly manipulating the state variables to align boat and country highlights more precisely. I\'m focusing on making the transitions feel cohesive and natural.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Entry**\n\nI\'m now integrating a sailboat animation and progress bar in `App.tsx` and refining the entry animation for the map section. I\'m focusing on the visual impact and timing of the sailboat animation. I\'m using `framer-motion` for a subtle, repeating bobbing and a downward-extending progress bar to introduce the visual journey. I\'m thinking about how the text will appear relative to these visuals.\n\n\n**Reflecting On Navigation Flow**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the boat navigation integrates between map and analysis sections. I\'m considering using `useScroll` to synchronize the boat\'s y position in `AnalysisSection.tsx` with scroll progress. I\'m evaluating two approaches: a shared state or independent scroll-based animations for the boat in each section. I\'m leaning toward a combined approach with `AnalysisSection` managing its progress bar and boat movement relative to scroll, while the map phase simplifies its transition. I aim for smooth continuity in the navigation experience, and the goal is a seamless visual journey.\n\n\n"
